      "parameters": {
        "mode": "runOnceForAllItems",
        "language": "javaScript",
        "jsCode": "const src = $(\"Generate Submission ID\").item.json;\nconst NEGATIVE_PROMPT = `extra people, human figures, pets, animals, invented furniture, new doorways, new rooms, new wall art, new decor, extra windows, warped architecture, duplicated columns, distorted geometry, morphing walls, glass reflections, mirror artifacts, ghosting, flat sliding image, static crop zoom, digital zoom artifact, camera pulling back beyond the original photo's edges, wider framing than the original photo, revealing space beyond the original frame, watermark, text overlay, blurry, low quality artifacts`;\nconst prompts = {\n  zoomIn: `Camera starts exactly at the framing of the original photo, unchanged, then performs a slow, smooth cinematic push-in toward the center of the room, continuing to advance gently. The motion should have a natural sense of depth — foreground elements move and grow slightly more than elements further back, giving the push-in a subtle three-dimensional feel rather than a flat, static image zoom. Keep this effect subtle and natural, not exaggerated. Camera stays locked on-axis, moving forward only, no lateral movement. Preserve exact room contents, furniture, layout, lighting, and architecture from the source image — do not add, invent, or hallucinate any objects, doors, windows, reflections, or elements not visible in the original photo. Vertical lines on walls, windows, and door frames stay perfectly straight throughout. Realistic depth of field, sharp focus maintained on foreground and midground. Natural shadow behavior as the camera moves closer. Professional real estate videography quality, steady and clean, 5–6 seconds.`,\n  panRight: `Camera starts exactly at the framing of the original photo, unchanged, then performs a very slow, subtle pan to the right combined with a gentle zoom in — gradually tightening the frame while drifting the visible window to the right, staying entirely within content already visible in the original photo. The framing must never extend past the right edge of the original photo; if approaching that edge, ease off the movement rather than reveal any new space. Do not reveal an entire new area of the room, a new doorway, new furniture, or a new room beyond what is already visible in the source image. Preserve exact room contents, furniture, layout, lighting, and architecture — do not add, invent, or hallucinate any objects, doors, windows, people, reflections, or elements not visible in the original photo. Vertical lines on walls, windows, and door frames stay perfectly straight throughout. Natural parallax, foreground elements shift slightly faster than the background. Realistic depth of field and shadow behavior. Professional real estate videography quality, steady and clean, 5–6 seconds.`,\n  panLeft: `Camera starts exactly at the framing of the original photo, unchanged, then performs a very slow, subtle pan to the left combined with a gentle zoom in — gradually tightening the frame while drifting the visible window to the left, staying entirely within content already visible in the original photo. The framing must never extend past the left edge of the original photo; if approaching that edge, ease off the movement rather than reveal any new space. Do not reveal an entire new area of the room, a new doorway, new furniture, or a new room beyond what is already visible in the source image. Preserve exact room contents, furniture, layout, lighting, and architecture — do not add, invent, or hallucinate any objects, doors, windows, people, reflections, or elements not visible in the original photo. Vertical lines on walls, windows, and door frames stay perfectly straight throughout. Natural parallax, foreground elements shift slightly faster than the background. Realistic depth of field and shadow behavior. Professional real estate videography quality, steady and clean, 5–6 seconds.`,\n  zoomOut: `Camera starts exactly at the framing of the original photo, unchanged, then performs an extremely slow, subtle ease-back — a gentle release of tension rather than a dramatic reveal, as if settling back a small fraction from the exact starting frame. The framing must never extend beyond the boundaries of the original photo at any point — no new walls, space, or rooms may become visible beyond what the source image already shows. If a wider framing would require revealing anything not already present in the original photo, do not perform it; keep the movement minimal enough to stay entirely within the original photo's visible content throughout. The motion should feel calm and barely perceptible, cinematic, not a dramatic zoom-out. Preserve exact room contents, furniture, layout, lighting, and architecture from the source image — do not add, invent, or hallucinate any objects, doors, windows, reflections, or elements not visible in the original photo. Vertical lines on walls, windows, and door frames stay perfectly straight throughout. Realistic depth of field and shadow behavior. Professional real estate videography quality, steady and clean, 5–6 seconds.`,\n};\nreturn [1, 2, 3, 4].map(n => ({\n  json: {\n    photoIndex: n,\n    photoUrl: src[`photo${n}Url`],\n    movementId: src[`movement${n}`] || \"zoomIn\",\n    prompt: prompts[src[`movement${n}`]] || prompts.zoomIn,\n    negativePrompt: NEGATIVE_PROMPT,\n  },\n  pairedItem: { item: 0 },\n}));"
      }
